2. Business Requirement
2.1 Background

The MHMR organization receives data from multiple source systems on a daily basis. This data is used for operational reporting, analytics, regulatory reporting, and business decision-making. As the volume of data increased and the number of source tables grew, the existing data processing approach became difficult to maintain, monitor, and scale.

To address these challenges, a standardized data processing framework was required that could efficiently process daily data loads, identify changes, maintain historical records, and provide reliable audit information for operational monitoring.

The MHMR Data Processing Framework was developed on Microsoft Fabric Lakehouse using PySpark to meet these requirements while providing scalability, reliability, and ease of maintenance.

2.2 Business Problem

Prior to implementing the framework, several operational and technical challenges existed:

Daily data processing required significant manual monitoring.
Historical changes to records were not consistently maintained.
Identifying inserted, updated, and deleted records across daily loads was inefficient.
Recovery from failed executions required manual intervention.
Processing large numbers of tables individually increased execution time.
Limited audit information made troubleshooting difficult.
Missing execution dates required manual backfill processing.
The solution lacked a standardized framework that could be reused across multiple datasets.

These challenges increased operational effort, delayed data availability, and reduced visibility into pipeline execution.

2.3 Business Goals

The primary business goals of the MHMR Data Processing Framework are:

Automate daily data ingestion and processing.
Detect incremental data changes accurately.
Preserve complete historical records for business reporting and compliance.
Maintain the latest snapshot of business data for operational reporting.
Reduce overall execution time through parallel processing.
Enable automated recovery from transient failures.
Provide detailed execution logs for auditing and operational monitoring.
Support backfill processing for missed execution dates.
Deliver a scalable framework capable of processing hundreds of tables with minimal configuration.
2.4 Functional Requirements

The framework shall provide the following functionality:

Data Ingestion
Read source data from Raw tables.
Compare current Raw data with Previous Raw data.
Detect Inserts, Updates, and Deletes.
Load incremental changes into the Staging layer.
History Processing
Maintain historical versions of records using SCD Type 2.
Mark active and inactive records appropriately.
Preserve historical data without overwriting previous versions.
Snapshot Processing
Maintain the latest state of each record.
Replace outdated records with the most recent version.
Ensure one current version exists for each business key.
Logging and Auditing
Record execution status for every processed table.
Capture processing start time and end time.
Store record counts for Inserts, Updates, and Deletes.
Record failures and retry attempts.
Backfill Processing
Identify dates that have not been processed.
Process missing dates automatically in chronological order.
Prevent duplicate processing.
2.5 Non-Functional Requirements

The framework should satisfy the following quality attributes:

Performance
Process multiple tables in parallel.
Minimize execution time for daily loads.
Avoid unnecessary full-table processing.
Scalability
Support more than 500 source tables.
Allow new tables to be added with minimal code changes.
Reliability
Retry failed operations automatically.
Continue processing remaining tables even if one table fails.
Maintain detailed failure logs for troubleshooting.
Maintainability
Use reusable functions.
Support metadata-driven execution.
Keep business logic centralized.
Auditability
Capture detailed execution logs.
Maintain historical processing information.
Support operational monitoring and compliance requirements.
2.6 Expected Business Outcomes

After implementation, the framework provides the following business benefits:

Faster daily data availability.
Accurate incremental data processing.
Complete historical tracking of business records.
Reliable point-in-time snapshots for reporting.
Reduced manual operational effort.
Improved visibility into pipeline execution.
Simplified recovery from failures.
Standardized processing across all source tables.
Improved scalability for future business growth.
2.7 Success Criteria

The implementation will be considered successful when:

Daily data loads complete successfully within the expected execution window.
Inserts, Updates, and Deletes are accurately detected and processed.
History tables maintain complete SCD Type 2 records.
Snapshot tables always reflect the latest state of the data.
Logs accurately capture execution details for every table.
Failed executions can be recovered through reruns without data inconsistency.
Backfill processing correctly handles missed dates.
The framework scales efficiently as additional tables are onboarded.
